From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 22:42:21 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] ui/virtio-media: scan video out on weston planes via ISP or
 SAND

On the Raspberry Pi 4 the DomA video path (virtio-media decoder in the
DomD device model, NV12 converted to RGBA with a GL shader, then blitted
by qemu and composited again by weston) does not hold 1080p60. The code
comments state that the GL conversion (a TFU shadow copy of the
1920x1088 NV12 frame plus the scaling draw) stretches every V3D job, the
display blit included, from ~2.5 ms to 3-12 ms; the blit's GPU
completion goes from 5.7 ms (720p) to 12 ms and weston's flips drop from
60/s to ~33/s. The VPU itself decodes 1080p at 76 frames/s and runs the
ISP at 72 frames/s at the same time.

This patch moves the work off V3D in stages. Every stage of the new path
is a run-time switch, a file under /run that qemu checks with access()
at most once a second (so removing it turns the feature off without a
restart); with the file absent that stage is not used. A few supporting
changes, listed further below, are unconditional. There are no new
device properties; the SAND parts only act when the existing
virtio-media property host-sand (default 0) is set, and the GL import
used by the SAND catch-up needs the existing gpu-xfer property (default
on).

Data path, from the decoder to the screen:

  - Decoder CAPTURE buffer (CMA, NV12 or SAND NV12_COL128) is exported
    with VIDIOC_EXPBUF.
  - host-isp: the VideoCore ISP (bcm2835-codec-isp, /dev/video12)
    converts it to RGBA into a ring of 24 ISP-owned MMAP buffers (CMA),
    cropping the codec's 1088-line padding and composing into the
    guest's visible rectangle. The ISP cannot write the guest's virgl
    resource directly: vc_sm_cma needs physically contiguous dmabufs and
    a v3d BO is not.
  - Each converted frame is copied into the guest resource (a
    same-format full-surface copy that Mesa v3d turns into one TFU job,
    ~0.7 ms at 1280x720). The guest's DQBUF events are held in a FIFO,
    in decode order, until the ISP has returned the codec buffer and the
    copy is done.
  - isp-scanout: the ring buffer is published in a direct-scanout
    registry (ui/direct-scanout.c) keyed by the guest texture. At flush
    time the SDL display looks the scanout texture up and, when the
    entry is still current, attaches the dmabuf to the SDL window's
    wl_surface through zwp_linux_dmabuf_v1 instead of blitting and
    swapping, so weston can put it on a vc4 plane.
  - isp-skip-copy: while the display keeps hitting, the copy into the
    guest resource is skipped and only done (catch-up) when a flush
    misses.
  - sand-scanout / sand-skip-isp: the decoder's SAND buffer itself is
    published as a two-plane NV12 buffer with the Broadcom SAND128
    modifier, so weston scans it out without the ISP; the guest's QBUF
    of a buffer still on screen is deferred until wl_buffer.release.

Switches (/run/qemu-*) and their defaults when the file is absent:

  - media-isp: use the ISP for NV12/SAND CAPTURE frames (off). Decided
    once per decode session. A frame not back after 250 ms closes the
    ISP and that session falls back to the GL path; a full ring sends
    one frame the GL way.
  - isp-scanout: present a published ring buffer directly on the window
    surface (off). Falls back to the blit for a dmabuf scanout, y0_top,
    a scanout rectangle that is not the whole window, or active overlay
    planes. Commits are paced on wl_surface.frame.
  - isp-scanout-depth: frames that may wait for the frame callback, 1..6
    (default 4; older ones are dropped). The comment states 17% dropped
    at 2 and the best flip rate at 4.
  - isp-skip-copy: skip the copy into the guest resource while the last
    30 flushes were direct and the last hit is fresher than 100 ms
    (off). On a miss every skipped copy is made and waited for
    before the display samples the texture; at most 12 copies are
    pending.
  - sand-scanout: publish the decoder's SAND buffer instead of the ISP
    ring (off). Once weston refuses such a buffer it is not offered
    again. At most 4 guest QBUFs are deferred; beyond that the buffer
    goes to the decoder anyway.
  - sand-skip-isp: with sand-scanout and skip-copy active, do not run
    the ISP for a frame that goes straight to the screen (off). It
    requires media-isp and an open ISP session, so the first frame of a
    session always takes the ISP. The GL conversion is still imported so
    a miss can fill the guest resource later.
  - media-sg-rw: copy guest scatter-gather buffers with
    dma_memory_read/write through the mapcache instead of mapping and
    unmapping each entry (off).
  - fb-invalidate: in egl_fb_blit(), invalidate a destination the blit
    fully covers instead of clearing it, but only when v3d will draw the
    blit (scaled or flipped) (off). This saves a separate clear job and
    a full tile load in Mesa v3d.

Other changes that are always on: the SAND luma rows are derived from
the column height (1632 -> 1088 for 1080p, 1088 -> 720 for 720p) instead
of col * 2 / 3; where a SAND column of ALIGN(h, 16) * 3 / 2 lines is not
a multiple of 32 (720p), the codec is asked for a frame padded to
ALIGN(luma, 64) rows (column 1152) and the guest still sees the
picture's own height, else plain NV12; the SDL blit copies only the
guest's scanout rectangle (1280x720 of a 1280x726 video buffer);
egl_fb_blit_rect() is added; three latency counters are added to the
existing QEMU_LAT_DIAG output (SDL blit, swap, ISP qbuf->dqbuf).

Measured effects stated in the code comments: with the CMA buffer on a
weston overlay plane, V3D load 0 and 60 fps; with a commit per flush the
HDMI got 45 flips/s of ~57 guest frames, hence the frame-callback
pacing; an 8-slot ring left about half the frames stale (49% direct), 16
slots still ~1.4% stale at depth 4, hence 24; SAND output costs the VPU
11.2 ms per 1080p frame against 15 ms with the firmware's NV12
conversion.

Adds ui/linux-dmabuf-unstable-v1-protocol.c and
ui/linux-dmabuf-unstable-v1-client-protocol.h, generated with
wayland-scanner 1.24.0. They are built only when wayland-client is
found; without it the direct scanout functions are stubs.

Left for later (2026-10-27, Raspberry Pi 4): the CAPTURE ring is
allocated whole (24 frames, about 88 MB of CMA at 720p) on the main
loop with the BQL held whenever a capture session starts, and a
session also starts when a WebView's player switches quality;
43 ms (480p) and 72 ms (720p) holds were seen there, inferred from the
log order. Allocate it lazily or off the main loop (a comment at
VIRTIO_MEDIA_ISP_RING says the same).

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [Raspberry Pi 4 specific (bcm2835 ISP, SAND, vc4 planes), run-time experiment switches]
